# The login worker's own database connection (server/README.md "Account passwords", "Login rush on a busy machine").
# Load test finding: the two login worker threads of 0014 used the engine's one database connection (Database::
# getInstance, one recursive mutex) with the dispatcher, so in a login rush every character list and every game login
# check queued behind the game's queries, and behind whole transactions (DBTransaction holds the lock from BEGIN to
# COMMIT: a player save). Half the logins of 398 bots 0.15 s apart took over 11 s.
#   * Each worker thread opens a connection of its own at its first login (a thread_local Database, the same class as
#     the main connection and as DatabaseTasks' own: the same automatic reconnect and query retry rules) and closes it
#     when the thread ends (PasswordWorker::shutdown joins them). Two more connections to the database server.
#   * IOLoginData::loginserverAuthentication / gameworldAuthentication take the connection as a parameter: the account
#     row, the character list, the character check and the password re-hash UPDATE (one autocommit statement) run on
#     it. A DBResult is read and freed on the worker thread before the task ends; nothing of it reaches the dispatcher
#     (the Account is copied out). No transaction on this path (DBTransaction always uses the main connection).
#   * When the worker cannot connect, the login uses the shared connection as before (one console warning) and the
#     next try comes 30 s later.
# Untouched: every query of the dispatcher and of Lua (the main connection), DatabaseTasks, the database class itself.
# Applies after 0001-0028.
